1
00:00:00,498 --> 00:00:03,929
Workday Inventory connects receiving, stock movements, and

2
00:00:03,929 --> 00:00:05,197
financial records.

3
00:00:05,363 --> 00:00:08,243
This prototype explores how visual evidence could

4
00:00:08,243 --> 00:00:10,638
help a receiving team review a delivery.

5
00:00:10,792 --> 00:00:12,880
A barcode identifies the item.

6
00:00:13,035 --> 00:00:14,579
A short video adds a view of

7
00:00:14,579 --> 00:00:16,250
its packaging and handling.

8
00:00:16,405 --> 00:00:18,444
In this clip, the carton is opened.

9
00:00:18,599 --> 00:00:22,562
Jev Omni evaluates four possible observations directly,

10
00:00:22,618 --> 00:00:24,143
and ranks opening first.

11
00:00:24,297 --> 00:00:26,483
The percentages compare those answers;

12
00:00:26,515 --> 00:00:28,554
the source stays beside the result.

13
00:00:28,709 --> 00:00:30,503
Now we add the business context.

14
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:32,349
The same photo could belong to an

15
00:00:32,349 --> 00:00:35,192
authorized contents inspection, where an open box

16
00:00:35,192 --> 00:00:36,128
is expected.

17
00:00:36,282 --> 00:00:37,924
Or it could be submitted as proof

18
00:00:37,924 --> 00:00:39,768
of the package's arrival condition.

19
00:00:39,922 --> 00:00:42,067
In that case, our example rule asks

20
00:00:42,067 --> 00:00:44,089
for an earlier photo, before the box

21
00:00:44,089 --> 00:00:44,853
was opened.

22
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:47,361
The prototype keeps the selected action, the

23
00:00:47,361 --> 00:00:50,270
supplied rule, and the original evidence together.

24
00:00:50,424 --> 00:00:52,336
A receiver can review the packet and

25
00:00:52,336 --> 00:00:54,812
request the missing view before continuing the

26
00:00:54,812 --> 00:00:56,152
receiving process.

27
00:00:56,319 --> 00:00:58,125
Here is how the technology works.

28
00:00:58,280 --> 00:01:01,540
TypeSafe's original Jev turns text and structured

29
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,750
questions into typed decisions.

30
00:01:03,905 --> 00:01:06,491
Jev Omni is an independent open model

31
00:01:06,491 --> 00:01:09,076
with a similar decision interface and multimodal

32
00:01:09,076 --> 00:01:09,853
inputs.

33
00:01:10,008 --> 00:01:12,709
Our video path samples sixteen frames.

34
00:01:12,863 --> 00:01:15,437
Those images enter a vision encoder, then

35
00:01:15,437 --> 00:01:17,839
a shared language backbone processes them with

36
00:01:17,839 --> 00:01:20,993
the question, context, and candidate answers.

37
00:01:21,148 --> 00:01:23,733
A classification head scores the options in

38
00:01:23,733 --> 00:01:25,074
one forward pass.

39
00:01:25,241 --> 00:01:28,010
Softmax converts those scores into the percentages

40
00:01:28,010 --> 00:01:28,898
we display.

41
00:01:29,064 --> 00:01:31,037
This avoids generating an answer word by

42
00:01:31,037 --> 00:01:31,630
word.

43
00:01:31,797 --> 00:01:33,709
The result is easy for software to

44
00:01:33,709 --> 00:01:36,177
route and for a reviewer to inspect.

45
00:01:36,331 --> 00:01:38,819
The important evaluation question is whether that

46
00:01:38,819 --> 00:01:41,202
decision is supported by the evidence.

47
00:01:41,356 --> 00:01:43,635
In this restricted crop, the model gave

48
00:01:43,635 --> 00:01:46,160
closed carton eighty percent, although the full

49
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:47,562
closure was out of view.

50
00:01:47,716 --> 00:01:49,775
A strong score can still accompany weak

51
00:01:49,775 --> 00:01:50,540
evidence.

52
00:01:50,706 --> 00:01:52,586
That case stays in the test set.

53
00:01:52,753 --> 00:01:54,983
The seven-second clip took about three point

54
00:01:54,983 --> 00:01:58,623
three seconds, including preprocessing and local GPU

55
00:01:58,623 --> 00:01:59,449
inference.

56
00:01:59,603 --> 00:02:01,111
Image calls took around a quarter of

57
00:02:01,111 --> 00:02:01,826
a second.

58
00:02:02,054 --> 00:02:04,579
For a receiving workflow, the opportunity is

59
00:02:04,579 --> 00:02:07,594
a compact evidence check: identify the item,

60
00:02:07,594 --> 00:02:10,241
inspect the visible condition, apply the capture

61
00:02:10,241 --> 00:02:12,324
context, and give the receiver a clear

62
00:02:12,324 --> 00:02:13,211
next step.

63
00:02:13,378 --> 00:02:16,356
The learning repository includes the prompts, recorded

64
00:02:16,356 --> 00:02:19,260
results, and this review prototype so others

65
00:02:19,260 --> 00:02:21,140
can explore the same questions.
